"The letters 'Ha Mim' at the beginning of Surah al-Mu'min, according to some scholars, indicate the names ar-Rahman and ar-Rahim, or serve as an oath by these names. It has also been narrated that these letters were recited as 'Ar-Rahman'. The letters 'Ha Mim' have also been interpreted as the beginning of Hamd (praise) and the middle of the name Muhammad, and it has been said to mean 'O Muhammad'. However, the majority stated that these are names of the Quran or of the surahs, and therefore, bearing characteristics such as 'alamiyyah' (proper noun) and 'ta'nith' (femininity), they are diptote (ghayr munsarif).\n\nThe angels who carry the Throne (Hamalat al-Arsh) are archangels, and their number is stated to be eight in Surah al-Haqqah. In some narrations, there is also the view that they are four today and will be eight on the Day of Resurrection. Furthermore, there are angels who surround the Throne; these are called 'Kerubiyyun' (Cherubim). Kerubiyyun means the angels closest to Allah, and according to some, they are only the angels who carry the Throne. Ibn Sina, on the other hand, stated that the Kerubiyyun angels are the commanders of the highest sphere, while the angels who carry the Throne are the Amilun (active) angels.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, it will be cried out to the disbelievers: 'Allah's hatred toward you is greater than your hatred toward yourselves; for when you were called to faith, you used to disbelieve.' They will say: 'Our Lord! You made us die twice and gave us life twice. Now we have recognized our sins. Is there, then, any way out?' In response, it will be said: 'This punishment is upon you for this reason: when Allah alone was invoked, you disbelieved; but when partners were associated with Him, you believed. So the judgment belongs to Allah, the Most High, the Most Great.'\n\nAllah is the One Who shows His signs and sends down provision for you from the heaven. Yet none understands this except those who turn to Him with heart and mind. So call upon Allah, dedicating religion purely to Him, even if the disbelievers dislike it. Allah is the Exalter of ranks, the Lord of the Throne. He sends down the spirit (the angel) by His command upon whomever He wills, to warn of the terror of the Day of Meeting. On that Day, it will be asked: 'Whose is the dominion this Day?' In response, it will be said: 'It belongs to Allah, the One, the Subduer.' On that Day, every soul will be recompensed for what it earned, and there will be no injustice.\n\nA believer from Pharaoh's family who concealed his faith opposed those who wished to kill Moses (Musa), saying: 'Will you kill a man merely because he says, \"My Lord is Allah,\" when he has come to you with clear proofs from your Lord? If he is a liar, his lie will be against him; but if he is truthful, some of that with which he threatens you will strike you.' Warning his people, this individual reminded them that the life of this world is only a temporary enjoyment and that the Hereafter is the enduring abode, calling them to salvation. Pharaoh, paying no heed to these admonitions, wished to build a tower and reach the pathways of the heavens to look upon the God of Moses, but his plot came to naught.\n\nWhile the believer was calling his people to salvation, they were calling him to the Fire. This appeal demonstrated that standing up against Pharaoh was both legitimate and necessary for salvation.\n\nAllah protected that believer from the plots of Pharaoh's family. Pharaoh and his troops suffered an evil punishment in this world. In the life of the grave as well, they are exposed to the Fire morning and evening and tormented, and on the Day of Resurrection, they will be cast into the severest punishment.\n\nAllah helps His messengers and those who believe, both in this world and in the Hereafter. On that Day, offering excuses will not benefit the wrongdoers; upon them is the curse, and for them is the evil abode. Moses was given guidance, and the Children of Israel were made to inherit the Torah. The Prophet was commanded patience, seeking forgiveness, and glorification.\n\nThose who dispute concerning the signs of Allah without any proof have in their breasts nothing but an arrogance that they will never attain. Regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse, there are two narrations: the polytheists of Quraysh, or the Jews awaiting the Dajjal. Both groups denied the truth out of their arrogance.\n\nThe creation of the heavens and the earth is greater than the creation of humankind. The blind and the seeing are not equal, nor are those who believe and do righteous deeds equal with the evildoer. The Hour will certainly come. Allah has said, 'Call upon Me; I will respond to you.' Those who are too proud to worship Him will enter Hell.\n\nTheir faith at the moment they saw Allah's wrath did not benefit them. This is the established law of Allah regarding His servants. It is at this point that the disbelievers were lost in ruin. That is to say, repentance and faith shown when the punishment arrives do not save a person; because for faith to be accepted, one must believe in the unseen and submit before witnessing the punishment.\n\nThese verses aim to remind of Allah's blessings and to encourage travel. Specifically, the creation of riding beasts provides humans with both transportation and other benefits. This reminder serves as a preparation for the call in verse 82 below: 'Have they not traveled through the earth and observed?'\n\nWhen the messengers came to them with clear proofs and miracles, the disbelievers relied 'in the name of knowledge' on the knowledge, crafts, and philosophy they possessed. Because of this reliance, they persisted in refusing to acknowledge the clear signs brought by the messengers.\n\nAccepting their own intellect and accumulated knowledge as absolute truth, they belittled the divine message. However, the miracles of the messengers and the clear signs they conveyed are present in every era, but the arrogant do not wish to see them.\n\nHere, people are urged to see the end of past communities and take heed. Traveling through the earth and observing the fate of those who disbelieved serves as a warning and a lesson for those who believe."